 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive density control originated with the introduction of 3D Gaussian Splatting by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [Parent]. The original method introduced heuristic-based splitting and cloning based on gradient magnitude and opacity thresholds to manage the population of Gaussians [13466-densification-and-splitting.md]. Subsequent research has expanded this family to include probabilistic approaches like MCMC [7852-3dgs-mcmc.md], memory-optimized layouts [9585-locality-preserving-densification.md], and multi-view consistency checks [17168-multi-view-consistent-densification.md]. These strategies are widely used in novel view synthesis, SLAM, and 3D generation pipelines to ensure efficient and high-fidelity scene representation.
+The concept of adaptive densification originated with the foundational 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1], which introduced the heuristic of cloning and splitting Gaussians based on accumulated positional gradients and opacity thresholds. This approach was designed to automatically adjust the density of primitives to match the complexity of the scene geometry and appearance. Subsequent developments have expanded this core mechanism to address specific challenges such as dynamic scenes [S1], memory efficiency [S5], multi-view consistency [S1, 15793-multi-view-consistency-and-scoring.md], and probabilistic sampling [S1][S4]. The method is widely used in novel view synthesis, SLAM, and 3D generation pipelines where high-fidelity reconstruction from sparse or dense image sets is required.
 
